WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
Marcus is comparing permanent policies and notices that several names include

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.580
the word variable. In variable life, the owner can allocate

3
00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:14.820
policy value among the offered investment options in a separate account.

4
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:19.600
Results depend on the selected investments as well as policy funding

5
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.640
and charges. The owner bears the specified investment risk and can

6
00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:30.400
lose value. This differs from a conventional fixed design's stated crediting

7
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.640
guarantees. A separate account is not the same as an indexed

8
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.960
interest formula. Ask what is invested, what can change,

9
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.820
and which benefit, if any, has a contractual minimum.

10
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.980
Similar product names do not establish identical risk.

11
00:00:47.948 --> 00:00:53.728
A traditional scheduled-premium variable whole life design combines

12
00:00:53.728 --> 00:00:58.248
fixed required premiums with investment-dependent cash values.

13
00:00:58.828 --> 00:01:03.528
It commonly includes a stated minimum death benefit if the required

14
00:01:03.528 --> 00:01:06.568
premiums and other conditions are satisfied.

15
00:01:07.488 --> 00:01:11.628
That minimum does not guarantee the cash value or every illustrated

16
00:01:11.628 --> 00:01:13.688
increase in the death benefit.

17
00:01:14.388 --> 00:01:16.268
Read the actual policy.

18
00:01:16.788 --> 00:01:21.398
In a teaching contract, the minimum death benefit is two hundred

19
00:01:21.398 --> 00:01:26.188
thousand dollars while investment results can produce additional protection.

20
00:01:26.988 --> 00:01:31.208
A decline in investment value does not by itself rewrite that

21
00:01:31.208 --> 00:01:36.328
minimum, but loans, missed premiums, or other contract

22
00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:39.528
provisions still need separate consideration.

23
00:01:40.148 --> 00:01:41.828
Identify the precise promise.

24
00:01:42.514 --> 00:01:47.334
Variable universal life combines investment options with the flexible-premium

25
00:01:47.334 --> 00:01:49.074
structure of universal life.

26
00:01:49.634 --> 00:01:54.394
Marcus may adjust payments within its terms, but policy deductions continue.

27
00:01:55.334 --> 00:01:58.714
Poor returns and reduced funding can work together to deplete available

28
00:01:58.714 --> 00:02:03.654
value. A specific no-lapse feature may provide protection if its conditions

29
00:02:03.654 --> 00:02:07.634
are met; the product name alone does not supply that guarantee.

30
00:02:07.634 --> 00:02:13.014
In comparing variable whole life with variable universal life, separate

31
00:02:13.014 --> 00:02:15.874
two questions. Who bears investment risk?

32
00:02:16.654 --> 00:02:20.674
Both variable designs expose the owner to the specified investment results.

33
00:02:21.654 --> 00:02:23.214
How are required payments structured?

34
00:02:24.294 --> 00:02:27.654
Scheduled premiums and flexible funding describe different designs.

35
00:02:28.502 --> 00:02:33.742
Use a simplified account containing ten thousand dollars, all allocated

36
00:02:33.742 --> 00:02:35.302
to one investment option.

37
00:02:36.102 --> 00:02:41.022
Assume no additional premium and a ten percent investment decline before

38
00:02:41.022 --> 00:02:42.942
an assumed four-hundred-dollar deduction.

39
00:02:44.082 --> 00:02:47.802
The value first falls to nine thousand dollars and then to

40
00:02:47.802 --> 00:02:48.882
eight thousand six hundred.

41
00:02:49.762 --> 00:02:54.202
These are teaching assumptions, not a forecast or a real policy's

42
00:02:54.202 --> 00:02:59.662
calculation order. The example shows why investment performance and insurance

43
00:02:59.662 --> 00:03:01.702
charges must both be considered.

44
00:03:02.642 --> 00:03:05.822
It does not determine the death benefit, which follows a separate

45
00:03:05.822 --> 00:03:10.042
provision. A policy can have an important death-benefit guarantee

46
00:03:10.982 --> 00:03:13.882
while its variable account loses value.

47
00:03:14.487 --> 00:03:18.007
Elena's family needs change after a mortgage is repaid.

48
00:03:19.147 --> 00:03:22.607
An adjustable life contract can permit changes to the face amount,

49
00:03:22.787 --> 00:03:26.947
premium, payment period, or protection plan within its rules.

50
00:03:28.127 --> 00:03:30.627
Changing one element can change the others.

51
00:03:31.567 --> 00:03:35.967
For example, keeping a lower premium may require less protection or

52
00:03:35.967 --> 00:03:38.667
a different protection period under the stated plan.

53
00:03:40.327 --> 00:03:44.247
Increasing the insured amount may require evidence of insurability.

54
00:03:45.607 --> 00:03:49.247
An ordinary adjustment does not automatically replace the insured with a

55
00:03:49.247 --> 00:03:53.947
different person. Some contemporary products use adjustable in a

56
00:03:53.947 --> 00:03:58.787
universal-life name. Read the actual mechanics instead of assuming that the

57
00:03:58.787 --> 00:04:01.227
word adjustable means unrestricted changes.

58
00:04:01.976 --> 00:04:07.176
Before a variable-life sale, explain the prospectus, investment

59
00:04:07.176 --> 00:04:12.276
options, expenses, surrender charges, and funding

60
00:04:12.276 --> 00:04:15.616
risks in language the applicant understands.

61
00:04:15.996 --> 00:04:20.456
Variable products involve securities requirements in addition to insurance

62
00:04:20.456 --> 00:04:25.736
authority. An insurance license alone does not grant every securities

63
00:04:25.736 --> 00:04:30.696
permission. Do not describe a variable policy as an ordinary bank

64
00:04:30.696 --> 00:04:33.396
savings account or promise a future return.

65
00:04:34.336 --> 00:04:39.316
For an existing adjustable policy, explain the requested change and document

66
00:04:39.316 --> 00:04:41.716
its acceptance through the insurer's process.

67
00:04:42.696 --> 00:04:45.376
Marcus and Elena should be able to state what is guaranteed,

68
00:04:46.276 --> 00:04:49.976
what can vary, what they must pay, and how their chosen

69
00:04:49.976 --> 00:04:51.236
protection could end.
